      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.069,
            0.156,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "108"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.068,
            0.477,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari |Grade 9| Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.17
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 9:** The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of the circle is double the angle subtended by the arc at any point on the circle outside the arc."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.202,
            0.415,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.165,
                0.401,
                0.398,
                0.434
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.21: Angle subtended by arc AFB"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.216,
            0.874,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": "**Given:** AFB is an arc. ∠ACB is the angle subtended by arc AFB at centre C. D is a point on the circle outside arc AFB (see Fig. 5.21)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.299,
            0.875,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": "We will assume for now that D is such that DC when extended cuts the circle at some point E on arc AFB, as shown."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.382,
            0.872,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "We will consider the other case later."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.458,
            0.437,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": "**To show:** $\\angle BCA = 2 \\angle BDA$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.485,
            0.885,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": "**Why is this true?** Join D to C and extend DC to cut the circle at a point E on arc AFB. Now ∆DCB is isosceles with $CB = CD$ $\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle\\mathrm{CBD}=\\angle\\mathrm{CDB}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.553,
            0.869,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": "∠BCE is the exterior angle of $\\Delta \\mathrm { B C D } ,$ By the exterior angle theorem,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.341,
            0.578,
            0.693,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": "\\angle BCE = \\angle CBD + \\angle CDB = 2\\angle BDC.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.605,
            0.722,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, ∆ADC is an isosceles triangle with $\\mathrm{CA}=\\mathrm{CD}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.633,
            0.192,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.631,
            0.501,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": "\\angle CAD = \\angle CDA",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.659,
            0.871,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": "$\\angle \\mathrm { A C E }$ is the exterior angle of $\\Delta \\mathrm { A D C } .$ By the exterior angle theorem,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.339,
            0.685,
            0.695,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": "\\angle ACE = \\angle CAD + \\angle CDA = 2\\angle CDA.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.712,
            0.73,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "Now $\\angle BCA = \\angle BCE + \\angle ECA,$ and $\\angle BDA = \\angle BDE + \\angle EDA$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.739,
            0.228,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "Hence"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.34,
            0.738,
            0.728,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "\\angle BCA = 2 ( \\angle BDC + \\angle CDA ) = 2 \\angle BDA.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.765,
            0.193,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.339,
            0.764,
            0.517,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": "\\angle BCA = 2\\angle BDA.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.792,
            0.887,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "The explanation we have given does not work if the situation is as shown in Fig. 5.22, where the extension of DC meets the circle at a point E outside the arc AFB. We must rework the explanation for this case."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
